  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "not stated",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper compares a different sharpness-aware training method with SAM and vanilla training, reporting test performance and a Hessian-based flatness measure. It does not test the specific conditions or measurements named in V1–V8.",
    "gate_note": "Rows report the paper's own test accuracy results for mSAM versus SAM and vanilla training, plus its measured largest Hessian eigenvalues. Cited results from prior work and runtime results are not included.",
    "test_reason": "The study uses the broader flatness–generalization hypothesis to motivate mSAM and evaluates its own method. It tests none of V1–V8. In particular, its unscaled largest Hessian eigenvalue and held-out accuracy do not measure V7's magnitude-aware perturbation tolerance or training–test gap. Varying micro-batch shards at fixed effective batch size does not test V5.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate: the authors conclude that mSAM improves performance over SAM across the tested tasks and architectures, with the size of the improvement depending on the setting. Smaller largest Hessian eigenvalues on two CIFAR-100 CNNs suggest flatter solutions as a possible explanation; a formal explanation remains future work.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study trains with mSAM, averaging updates from adversarial perturbations across mini-batch shards, and compares it with SAM and vanilla methods; this is not the recipes specified in the claim versions.",
    "method_sensitivity": "On CIFAR 100, accuracy generally improves as m increases up to m ≈ 32, while larger m gives worse performance or marginal improvements. The authors state that the optimal interaction between m and batch size remains an open question.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "mSAM has higher reported mean held-out performance than SAM and vanilla training in all listed CIFAR and GLUE comparisons. Improvements vary substantially, and the authors caution about variability on smaller NLP datasets.",
        "proposed_claim": "mSAM can improve held-out performance over SAM and vanilla training on these image-classification and language fine-tuning tasks.",
